<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>8.3.3: Solving More Complex Quadratic Equations</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02118</md:content-id>
    <md:title>8.3.3: Solving More Complex Quadratic Equations</md:title>
    <md:uuid>fd5bf3de-b3c8-470a-9d88-9f72ae640b5c</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Activity (5 minutes)</title>
<para>In this activity, students will use what they have learned about the square root property to reason the solutions to
  more complex quadratic equations.</para>
<para>The variable being squared is now an expression being squared, but the principles used to solve the equation remain
  the same. Students will use the square root property to identify two different solutions.</para>
<title>Launch</title>
<para>Read the activity opening together as a class. Ask students why solving the new equation might be more complex. (The
  variable being squared is now an expression being squared.)</para>
<para>As a hint for the first exercise, tell students to take the square root of both sides as previously done. This gives
  \( \pm \sqrt{144}=(n+1)\).</para>
<para>Students might then reason:</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent">
  <item> Some number plus 1 equals the positive square root of 144. </item>
  <item> Some number plus 1 equals the negative square root of 144. </item>
</list>
<div class="os-raise-extrasupport">
  <note>
    <para>Support for English Language Learners<title>Support for English Language Learners</title></para>
    <para>MLR 7 Compare and Connect: Representing, Conversing<title>MLR 7 Compare and Connect: Representing, Conversing</title></para>
  </note>
  <div class="os-raise-extrasupport-body">
    <para>Use this routine to prepare students for the whole-class discussion. At the appropriate time, invite student pairs to create a visual display of their process for solving the first equation and the second equation. Displays may include a list of steps, a flow chart, etc. Allow students time to quietly circulate and analyze at least two other visual displays in the room. Give students quiet think time to consider how the processes are similar and how they are different. Next, ask students to return to their partner and discuss what they noticed. Listen for and amplify observations that connect the parentheses with adding another step to the solution/thinking process. This will help students make connections between algebraic representations of quadratic functions.</para>
    <para class="os-raise-text-italicize">Design Principle(s): Optimize output; Cultivate conversation</para>
    <para class="os-raise-extrasupport-title">Learn more about this routine</para>
    <para>
      <link url="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=PF8fRA107OA;&amp;rel=0">View the instructional video</link>
      and
      <link url="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/cd42f6dfe23e48d50c003bbdc11d758914a4acf0">follow along with the materials</link>
      to assist you with learning this routine.
    </para>
    <para class="os-raise-extrasupport-title">Provide support for students</para>
    <para>
      <link url="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/dd41dd1a4057402964bff003365e7e89158db868">Distribute graphic organizers</link>
      to the students to assist them with participating in this routine.
    </para>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="os-raise-extrasupport">
  <note>
    <para>Support for Students with Disabilities<title>Support for Students with Disabilities</title></para>
    <para>Representation: Internalize Comprehension<title>Representation: Internalize Comprehension</title></para>
  </note>
  <div class="os-raise-extrasupport-body">
    <para>Use color and annotations to illustrate student thinking. Consider highlighting the parts of the equations that are similar and the parts of the equations that are different for each equation. Then, highlight the parts of the process that are similar and the parts of the process that are different for each equation. Press students to use annotations to connect the highlighted parts for the equations and process.  </para>
    <para class="os-raise-text-italicize">Supports accessibility for: Visual-spatial processing </para>
  </div>
</div>


<title>Student Activity</title>
<para> Solving an equation of the form \(a(x−h)^2=k\) is a similar process to the equations we have previously
  solved.</para>
<para>Notice that the quadratic term, \(x\), in the original form \(ax^2= k\) is replaced with \((x−h)\).</para>
<para><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/8ad8594218d5b68532366828d0631179c3d22273"/></media></figure></para>
<para>Once the binomial is isolated, the square root property can be used. You can reason the solution by taking the square
  root and then appropriately isolating the variable using the two different square roots.</para>
<para>Solve the following quadratic equations. Explain or show your reasoning.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>\(144=(n+1)^2\)</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(n=11\) and \(n=-13\)</para>
<para>Because 12 and −12 are the two numbers we can square to make 144 and \(n+1\) is the number being squared, we
  need \(n\) to be 1 less than 12 or −12. So \(n=11\) and \(n=-13\).</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>\((u−6)^2= 64\)</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(u=14\) and \(u=-2\)</para>
<para>Since 8 and −8 are the two numbers we can square to make 64 and \(u−6\) is the number being squared, we
  need \(u\) to be 6 more than 8 or −8. So \(u=14\) and \(u=-2\).</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item>\((n−5)^2−30=70\)</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(n=15\) and \(n=-5\)</para>
<para>To isolate the binomial, add 30 to both sides of the equation. Since 10 and –10 are the two numbers we can
  square to make 100 and \(n-5\) is the number being squared, we need \(n\) to be 5 more than 10 or –10. So
  \(n=15\) and \(n=-5\).</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item>\(4(x+6)^2−5=31\)</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>\(x=-3\) and \(x=-9\)</para>
<para>To begin isolating the binomial, add 5 to both sides of the equation. Then divide both sides of the equation by 4.
  Since 3 and –3 are the two numbers we can square to make 9 and \(x+6\) is the number being squared, we need
  \(x\) to be 6 less than 3 or –3. So \(x=-3\) and \(x=-9\).</para>
<title>Student Facing Extension</title>
<title>Are you ready for more?</title>
<para>Solve the following quadratic equation. Be prepared to show your reasoning.</para>
<para>\((4x−3)^2+11=47\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Two solutions: \(x=\frac{9}{4}\) and \(x=−\frac{3}{4}\).</para>
<para>To isolate the binomial, subtract 11 from both sides of the equation. This leaves \((4x − 3)^2= 36\). When you
  take the square root of both sides, you end up with \(4x−3= \pm 6\). This leaves two equations to solve:</para>
<para>\(4x−3=6\;\;\;\;\;\;\;4x−3=−6\)</para>
<para>By solving each of them, you find the solutions \(x=\frac{9}{4}\) and \(x=−\frac34\).</para>
<title>Video: Solving More Complex Quadratic Equations</title>
<para>Watch the following video to learn more about how to solve a quadratic equation of the form \(a(x−h)^2=k\).</para>
<div class="os-raise-d-flex-nowrap os-raise-justify-content-center">
  <div class="os-raise-video-container"><video controls="true" crossorigin="anonymous">
      <source src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/ece9009efec5cf2b9b57449f601cd71c89a38156">
      <track default="true" kind="captions" label="On" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/c8ddf4586b97344ab135581706a8c62e3b75f204" srclang="en_us">
    </track></source></video></div>
</div>


<title>Activity Synthesis</title>
<para>This activity demonstrates a different way to use the square root property to solve a more complex equation.</para>
<para>After completing the activity, ask students to rewrite the square root property using the following starting
  expression:</para>
<para>If \((x+1)^2=k\), then ____________.</para>
<para>After some discussion, reveal the answer to be:</para>
<para>\(x+1=\sqrt{k} \quad \text { and } \quad x+1=-\sqrt{k} \rightarrow x=-1\pm \sqrt{k}\)</para>
<title>8.3.3: Self Check</title>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold"><emphasis>After the activity, students will answer the following question to check their understanding of the
    concepts explored in the activity.</emphasis></para>

<para class="os-raise-text-bold">QUESTION:</para>
<para>Solve the following quadratic equation.</para>
<para class="os-raise-noindent">\((x+3)^2−16=48\)</para>
<table class="os-raise-textheavytable">
  <thead>
    <row>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">Answers</entry>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">Feedback</entry>
    </row>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry>
        \(x=11\) and \(x=-5\)
      </entry>
      <entry>
        Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: The final step is subtracting 3 from –8 and 8, not
        adding. The answer is \(x=-11\) and \(x=5\).
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        \(x=-11\) and \(x=5\)
      </entry>
      <entry>
        That’s correct! Check yourself: Insert each value into the original equation.
        \(((-11)+3)^2−16=48\) is true, and \(((5)+3)^2−16=48\) is true.
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        \(x=-8\) and \(x=8\)
      </entry>
      <entry>
        Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: You forgot the final step of subtracting 3 from –8
        and 8. The answer is \(x=-11\) and \(x=5\).
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        \(x=5\)
      </entry>
      <entry>
        Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: When you take the square root of 64, remember that there
        are two possible answers, –8 and 8. Then subtract 3 from each answer. The answer is \(x=-11\) and
        \(x=5\).
      </entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>

<title>8.3.3: Additional Resources</title>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold"><emphasis>The following content is available to students who would like more support based on their experience with
    the self check. Students will not automatically have access to this content, so you may wish to share it with
    those who could benefit from it.</emphasis></para>
<title>Solving Quadratic Equations of the Form \(a(x - h)^2 = k\)</title>
<para>We can use the square root property to solve an equation of the form \(a(x-h)^2=k\) as well.</para>
<para>Notice that the quadratic term, \(x\), in the original form \(ax^2=k\) is replaced with \((x−h)\).</para>
<para>\(a {\style{color:red}x}^{2}=k \quad
  a{\style{color:red}(}{\style{color:red}x}\;{\style{color:red}-}\;{\style{color:red}h}{\style{color:red})}^{2}=k\)</para>
<para>Like before, the first step is to isolate the term that has the variable squared. In this case, a binomial is being
  squared.</para>
<para>Once the binomial is isolated, by dividing each side by the coefficient of \(a\), the square root property can be
  used on \((x−h)^2\).</para>
<para>Solve \(4(y−7)^2=48\).</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Isolate the quadratic term and make its coefficient 1.</para>
<para> So, divide both sides of the equation by 4.</para>
<para>\(4(y−7)^2=48\)
  \((y−7)^2=12\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Use the square root property on the binomial.</para>
<para>  \(y−7= \pm \sqrt{12}\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Simplify the radical by pulling out the factors that are perfect squares.</para>
<para>  Solve for \(y\). Rewrite the two solutions.</para>
<para>\(y−7= \pm \sqrt{12}\)
  \(y−7= \pm \sqrt{(4 \cdot 3})\)
  \(y−7= \pm 2 \sqrt{3}\)
  \(y =7 \pm 2 \sqrt{3}\)
  \(y =7+2 \sqrt{3}\), \(y=7−2 \sqrt{3}\) </para>
<para><emphasis>Step 4 </emphasis>
  Check the solutions.</para>
<para>\(\begin{array}{rrr} 4(y-7)^{2}=48 &amp; 4(y-7)^{2}=48 \\
  4({\style{color:red}7}\;{\style{color:red}+}\;{\style{color:red}2} \sqrt{{\style{color:red}3}}-7)^{2} \stackrel{?}{=}
  48 &amp; 4({\style{color:red}7}\;{\style{color:red}-}\;{\style{color:red}2} \sqrt{{\style{color:red}3}}-7)^{2}
  \stackrel{?}{=} 48 \\ 4(2 \sqrt{3})^{2} \stackrel{?}{=} 48 &amp; 4(-2 \sqrt{3})^{2} \stackrel{?}{=} 48 \\ 4(12)^{2}
  \stackrel{?}{=} 48 &amp; 4(12)^{2} \stackrel{?}{=} 48 \\ 48=48 {\style{color:red}\checkmark} &amp; 48=48
  {\style{color:red}\checkmark} \end{array} \)</para>
<title>Try It: Solving Quadratic Equations of the Form \(a(x-h)^2=k\)</title>
<para>Solve the following quadratic equations.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>\(3(a−3)^2=363\) </item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item> \(\frac{1}{2} (b+2)^2=200\) </item>
</list>
<para>Write down your answers, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
<title>Solution</title>
<para>Here is how to solve these quadratic equations of the form \(a(x-h)^2=k\):</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item> \(3(a−3)^2 =363\) 
    \((a−3)^2=121\)
    \(a−3= \pm 11\)
    \(a−3=11\)      \(a−3=-11\)
    \(a= 14\) and \(a=-8\)</item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item> \(\frac{1}{2}(b+2)^2=200\)
    \((b+2)^2=400\)
    \(b+2= \pm 20\)
    \(b+2=20\)      \(b+2=-20\)
    \(b=18\) and \(b=-22\)</item>
</list></section>
